{
 "id": "2020-bielinis-forest",
 "name": "Forest Discomfort Scale and Landscape Pleasantness (AEFES, Photo Rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Bielinis, E., Xu, J., & Omelan, A. A. (2020). A Novel Anti-Environmental Forest Experience Scale to Predict Preferred Pleasantness Associated with Forest Environments. Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 17:6731, doi:10.3390/ijerph17186731. Participants give demographics (gender, age group, place of living, education, material standing, activity), complete the 21-item Anti-Environmental Forest Experience Scale (discomfort from plants and litter, animals, disgusting things and insects in the forest, 1–7), then rate nine photographs (three urban views, three forest and three dense forest landscapes near Olsztyn, Poland, and in Helsinki), each on its own page in random order, on three 7-point word pairs (unpleasant–pleasant, I don't like it–I like it, ugly–beautiful) averaged into preferred pleasantness. Analysis as in the paper: AEFES subscale means, mean pleasantness per photograph and landscape type, correlations between subscales and pleasantness. Photographs: paper Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the Connectedness to Nature Scale and the spatial–symbolic domain of the Amoebic Self Scale, used in the paper for validity checks, are left out because they are separately published instruments; (2) the original was in Polish; the item wording here is the English item list in Table 3, and the AEFES instruction and response wording were reconstructed from the Methods; (3) the photographs come from the published figure and are cropped at the bottom to remove panel letters, so they are smaller and slightly cut; (4) a consent box was added and demographics share one page with it.",
 "author": "Bielinis, Xu & Omelan",
